-- title:
--   Black–Scholes market: augmented Brownian filtration, $S$, $D$ and the pricing kernel $Z$
-- statement:
--   Let $B=(B_t)_{t\ge0}$ be a one-dimensional Brownian motion on $(\Omega,\mathcal F,P)$. The **augmented natural filtration** of $B$ is
--
--   $$\mathcal F_t:=\sigma(B_s: s\le t)\vee\{N\in\mathcal F: P(N)=0\},\qquad t\ge0.$$
--
--   The market has a stock with price $S_t=S_0\exp((\mu-\sigma^2/2)t+\sigma B_t)$, the solution of $dS_t/S_t=\mu\,dt+\sigma\,dB_t$, and a money-market account with price $D_t=e^{rt}$. The unique **pricing kernel** is
--
--   $$Z_t=\exp\Big(-\theta B_t-\big(r+\tfrac{\theta^2}{2}\big)t\Big),\qquad \theta=\frac{\mu-r}{\sigma}.$$
--
--   **Formalization Note** The paper works on a "standard filtered probability space supporting a one-dimensional Brownian motion" and states that the market is complete; this is read as: the filtration is the augmented natural filtration of $B$ (completeness needs a Brownian filtration). The null sets added are the measurable null sets. The stock price is written in closed form.
-- source:
--   Tse–Zheng, Portfolio Selection, Periodic Evaluations and Risk Taking, SSRN 3971852 (June 20, 2023), §2, p.6 (filtered space, S, D) and eq. (1), p.7 (pricing kernel)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_PeriodicEval_Params

open MeasureTheory
open scoped NNReal

namespace PeriodicEval

/-- `ℱ` is the `P`-augmented natural filtration of `B`: for every `t`, `ℱ t` is generated by
`B s`, `s ≤ t`, together with the `P`-null sets. SSRN 3971852 (June 20, 2023), §2, p.6:
"a standard filtered probability space supporting a one-dimensional Brownian motion", in a
Black–Scholes economy that "is complete with a unique pricing kernel".

**Formalization Note** This is a reading of "standard filtered probability space": the
completeness of the market (replication, used in the proof of Theorem 1, p.44) needs a
Brownian filtration, and the augmentation lets null-set modifications of a predictable
process stay predictable. The null sets added are the measurable `P`-null sets of the ambient
σ-algebra, so `ℱ t` is a sub-σ-algebra of it. The hypothesis forces each `B s` to be
measurable (as `ℱ s` is below the ambient σ-algebra), and makes `B` an `ℱ`-Brownian motion. -/
def IsAugmentedNaturalFiltration {Ω : Type*} [m : MeasurableSpace Ω] (P : Measure Ω)
    (B : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) (ℱ : Filtration ℝ≥0 m) : Prop :=
  ∀ t : ℝ≥0, ℱ t =
    (⨆ s : ℝ≥0, ⨆ (_ : s ≤ t), MeasurableSpace.comap (B s) inferInstance) ⊔
      MeasurableSpace.generateFrom {N : Set Ω | MeasurableSet N ∧ P N = 0}

/-- The stock price `S_t = S₀ exp((μ − σ²/2)t + σB_t)`, the solution of `dS_t/S_t = μ dt + σ dB_t`,
SSRN 3971852 (June 20, 2023), §2, p.6.

**Formalization Note** The geometric Brownian motion is written in closed form. -/
noncomputable def S (p : Params) {Ω : Type*} (B : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) (t : ℝ≥0) (ω : Ω) : ℝ :=
  p.S₀ * Real.exp ((p.μ - p.σ ^ 2 / 2) * (t : ℝ) + p.σ * B t ω)

/-- The money-market price `D_t = e^{rt}`, SSRN 3971852 (June 20, 2023), §2, p.6. -/
noncomputable def D (p : Params) (t : ℝ≥0) : ℝ := Real.exp (p.r * (t : ℝ))

/-- The pricing kernel (1), SSRN 3971852 (June 20, 2023), p.7:
`Z_t = exp(−θB_t − (r + θ²/2)t)`. -/
noncomputable def Z (p : Params) {Ω : Type*} (B : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) (t : ℝ≥0) (ω : Ω) : ℝ :=
  Real.exp (-(p.θ * B t ω) - (p.r + p.θ ^ 2 / 2) * (t : ℝ))

end PeriodicEval


